#### Article 6

1. The Regent has the authority to establish the PUG Working Group.  
2. The establishment of the PUG Working Group as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.  
3. The Regent's Decree as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be valid for 5 (five) years.  
4. The management of the PUG Working Group consists of:
   a. chairperson by the head of the agency;  
   b. secretariat by the head of the office;  
   c. members including:
      1. head of PD;  
      2. technical team consisting of:
         a. apparatus who have attended training or education in gender-responsive budget analysis of at most 5 (five) persons.  
         b. representatives of university elements of at most 5 (five) persons;  
         c. press representatives of at most 5 (five) persons; and  
         d. representatives of non-governmental organizations that have an orientation scope in the field of gender of at most 5 (five) persons.

#### Article 7

The duties of the PUG Working Group include:

a. promoting and facilitating dialogue between work units within the scope of regional government;  
b. developing work networks in accordance with the main duties, functions and authorities given by the leadership in efforts to realize gender equality and justice;  
c. assigning the technical team to prepare the PUG RAD in order to realize gender equality and justice adjusted to the RPJMD periodization;  
d. elaborating and following up on policies and implementation programs to be gender responsive that are implicit and explicit in the RPJMD;  
e. coordinating the establishment of PUG Focal Points in each PD or work unit;  
f. promoting PUG in each PD;  
g. strengthening the capacity of PUG Focal Points in preparing gender-responsive budgets by the technical team;  
h. overseeing the preparation of gender-responsive planning and budgeting in each PD;  
i. including gender indicators in the guidelines for preparing the APBD;  
j. ensuring that gender-responsive budgets become a consideration in the discussion and determination of the APBD together with the DPRD;  
k. holding coordination meetings every 6 (six) months to ensure the implementation of PUG in the Region;  
l. preparing and submitting reports on PUG implementation to the regent every 6 (six) months; and  
m. holding coordination meetings before the preparation of the RKPD.

### Paragraph 4

PUG Focal Point

#### Article 8

1. The Regional Secretary has the authority to establish a PUG Focal Point within the Regional Secretariat.  
2. The establishment of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regional Secretary's Decree.  
3. The management of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
   a. person in charge by the regional secretary;  
   b. coordinator by an echelon IIB official in charge of governmental affairs in the field of law;  
   c. vice coordinator by an echelon IIIA official of each division in the regional secretariat except for the echelon IIB official as referred to in letter b; and  
   d. members by representatives of officials and/or staff from all divisions in the regional secretariat.

#### Article 9

1. The Head of PD is obliged to establish a PD PUG Focal Point.  
2. The establishment of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be by Head of PD's Decree.  
3. The management of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
   a. person in charge by the head of PD;  
   b. coordinator by an echelon III A official in charge of planning affairs at the PD;  
   c. Vice Coordinator by an Echelon IIIB Official who has competence in PUG; and  
   d. Members by representatives of Officials or staff from divisions and secretariat at the PD.

#### Article 10

1. The Hospital Director has the authority to establish a Hospital PUG Focal Point.  
2. The establishment of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Hospital Director's decree.  
3. The management of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
   a. person in charge by the hospital director;  
   b. coordinator by an echelon IIIB official in charge of hospital planning;  
   c. vice coordinator by an echelon IVA official who has competence in PUG; and  
   d. members by representatives of officials or staff in divisions or sections within the hospital.

#### Article 11

1. The Head of the Community Health Center has the authority to establish a Puskesmas PUG Focal Point.  
2. The establishment of the PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Head of Community Health Center's Decree.  
3. The management of the puskesmas PUG Focal Point consists of:
   a. person in charge by the Head of Puskesmas;  
   b. coordinator by an echelon IV B official in charge of Puskesmas planning; and  
   c. members consisting of Puskesmas staff.

#### Article 12

The duties of the PUG Focal Point include:

a. assisting policy makers of work units within the scope of duties, main tasks and functions of their agencies to take planned steps fully when there are gender gaps;  
b. encouraging and assisting work units to evaluate and improve mandates, policies, programs, projects, activities and budgets to be more gender responsive;  
c. facilitating the implementation of gender sensitivity training, gender analysis training and developing gender work networks with agencies or institutions or organizations and other work units, both government and non-government;  
d. seeking the implementation of gender analysis as one of the stages in every development process starting from the planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation stages;  
e. elaborating and following up on policies and development implementation programs to be gender responsive that are implicit in the PD Strategic Plan, PD Work Plan and RKPD;  
f. participating in various activities organized by the PUG Working Group and/or national PUG working group; and  
g. preparing activity reports periodically and reporting them to the regent through the chairperson of the PUG working group.

### Paragraph 5

Gender-based Regional Development Planning

#### Article 13

1. The Regional Government is responsible for preparing planning for policies, programs and development activities with a Gender perspective.  
2. The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of development planning, research and Regional development.  
3. The planning as referred to in paragraph (1) includes stages:
   a. Musrenbangdus;  
   b. Musrembangdes/kel;  
   c. Musrembangcam;  
   d. Musrenbang at the regional level;  
   e. preparation of RPJMD;  
   f. preparation of PD Strategic Plan;  
   g. preparation of PD Work Plan;  
   h. RKPD;  
   i. preparation of RAPBD;  
4. The planning as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through Gender analysis using the Gender Analysis Pathway method or other analytical methods.  
5. The implementation of Gender analysis on the RPJMD and PD Strategic Plan may cooperate with Higher Education institutions or other parties who have capability in their field.

#### Article 14

1. The Agency is responsible for preparing guidelines for gender-based development planning.  
2. The guidelines as referred to in paragraph (1) must be used in facilitating:
   a. Musrenbangdus;  
   b. Musrembangdes/kel;  
   c. Musrembangcam;  
   d. Musrenbang at the regional level;  
   e. preparation of RPJMD;  
   f. preparation of PD Strategic Plan; and  
   g. preparation of PD Work Plan, that is gender responsive.  
3. The preparation of guidelines as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with the PUG Working Group Technical Team.

#### Article 15

1. The Gender Analysis Pathway method as referred to in Article 13 paragraph (4) shall be used as a way to make planning in each PD.  
2. The method as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: 
   a. stage I analysis of gender-responsive policies;  
   b. stage II formulation of gender-responsive policies; 
   c. stage III gender-responsive action plan;  
   d. stage IV implementation of activities that have been prepared; and 
   e. stage V monitoring and evaluation of each stage and step taken.

### Paragraph 6

Budgeting
